          kind: 'para',
          text: 'In 1948, with the liberated camps still fresh in memory, the UN General Assembly adopted the Universal Declaration of Human Rights: thirty articles, no enforcement machinery, and a claim that still startles — rights belong to every person simply for being human. Everything since, the covenants, the committees, the regional courts, a vocabulary dictators must speak too, grows from that document. Human rights law after 1948 is the story of trying to give the Declaration teeth without a world government to bite with.',
